//? Title: Historical MuJoCo sorting benchmark
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

This is the historical MuJoCo 3.4.0 experiment and its retained results. The
standard datamunge packages now use the in-house engine. To rerun this reference,
build `nix build .#datamunge-mujoco` and supply its `lib/pkgconfig` directory via
`DATAMUNGE_CAD_PKG_CONFIG_PATH` when executing this block. The current native
experiment is in [playground|file:playground.mepml].

> Robot sorting with articulated contact physics and native RL

This benchmark compares **Q(λ), Double DQN, dueling Double DQN and clipped PPO**
using datamunge's native C++ learning backend. It selects a method and checkpoint
on independent validation streams, freezes that choice, then evaluates it on
held-out streams and renders a **20-second example** of the selected controller.
The former proxy-trained controller is evaluated as a historical transfer
reference and remains eligible for deployment if it wins independent validation. A neural controller is not guaranteed to win.

\image(figures/fruit_stream_physics_rl_protocol.svg)
\caption(The four fresh pipelines share a training curriculum, fixed motor options and frozen vision. Physical screening narrows checkpoints, independent actual-RGB validation selects the deployed policy, and held-out evaluation follows without reselection.)
\alttext(Four native learning methods feed physical screening, RGB validation, a frozen choice, held-out evaluation and a separately seeded rendered demonstration.)

>> The task and observation

Fruit enters a continuously moving conveyor under an independent **Poisson
process**, with random position, orientation, size, color and six shape prototypes
per class. The demonstration and evaluation rate is **0.12 fruit/s**. The belt
runs at **0.22 m/s**; fruit passing X=0.30 m unhandled is missed. Apples belong in
the red bin and bananas in the yellow bin. Outcomes require settling with bin
floor support. Completed fruit clears after two seconds; arrivals at the
eight-body capacity count as misses. Fruit still moving, held or falling at an exact
measurement boundary remains pending.

Every pipeline chooses among the same **five variable-duration options**: wait,
intercept the oldest fruit at either of two gates, or intercept the second-oldest
fruit at either gate. Feasible-action masks exclude absent targets, blocked
approaches and expired interception deadlines. Fixed IK options request approach,
descent, finger closure, transfer and release; **MuJoCo integrates the actual
motion**. A grasp requires sustained force from both fingers. Fruit remains a
free dynamic body during transport and can slip or fall. Releases preserve
velocity. Success requires bin-floor contact, low linear/angular speed and a
continuous 0.2-second settling interval. Empty-queue waits yield every 0.125
seconds while the actual wrist turns toward the infeed.

>> Physics audit and numerical checks

The previous simulation interpolated robot joints, attached fruit before finger
closure, carried it kinematically and zeroed release velocity. Its fast training
proxy further approximated conveyor transport and settling. Those mechanisms
could reward behavior that would fail under contact dynamics; earlier performance
figures are historical proxy-task results.

This experiment uses **MuJoCo 3.4.0**, with the pinned Seeed URDF's joint origins,
axes, limits, link masses, centers of mass and inertia tensors. Official collision
meshes become convex hulls; each concave finger pad is split into smaller hulls
to retain its opening. Apples use ellipsoids and bananas compound tapered
capsules. Gravity, joint coupling, self-collision except adjacent links, fruit
rotation, friction, contact forces and actuator effort limits are integrated.
The moving belt acts through tangential contact constraints; fruit velocity is
not assigned to belt speed. Rendering and the wrist camera use measured poses.

The task clock is 240 Hz and integration runs at **960 Hz** with full implicit-in-velocity
integration, elliptic friction cones and an iterative contact solver. Position
references drive force-limited rate servos. Requested rates are capped at
1.5 rad/s and 0.08 m/s, but **actual rates can exceed them** under contact and
backdriving. The report records actual peak rates, penetration and effort-limit
usage instead of treating requested rates as measured hard limits.

\image(figures/fruit_stream_physics_physics.svg)
\caption(A separate perfect-perception development stream checks four integration timesteps. Transport error uses the 1920 Hz trajectory as a numerical reference over world time 8–10 s; contact penetration is reported separately. Neither quantity establishes agreement with hardware.)
\alttext(Two panels show conveyor-position error and maximum contact penetration versus integration timestep.)

Unit checks cover free fall, momentum, rotation, moving-belt traction, bin support,
spawn/reset invariance and agreement between dynamics and rendered forward
kinematics. The 960 and 1920 Hz development runs have the same aggregate task
outcomes; contact trajectories and maximum penetration are not monotonic in the
step size. MuJoCo can enlarge the contact time constant at coarse timesteps for stability,
so this is a practical sensitivity check rather than a fixed-equation convergence
proof. Learned-policy streams below are separate from this development check.
A torque-free check also shows numerical damping: about 0.08% energy loss over
two seconds at 5.4 rad/s initial spin, but roughly 50% at an extreme 269 rad/s.
Stable rapid-tumbling trajectories are therefore not claimed to be accurate.

This is a **nominal rigid-body model, not a hardware-calibrated digital twin**.
Friction, damping, reflected motor inertia, finger mass and servo gains need
measurement. Fruit compliance, damage, motor electronics, latency, camera noise
and manufacturing tolerances remain approximations. The published hardware flyer
and pinned URDF also differ in some limits. Mesh fidelity alone cannot resolve
that revision uncertainty. See the [physics audit|file:figures/fruit_stream_physics_audit.md]
for model assumptions, references and calibration requirements.

The tabular methods use the existing **1,680-state abstraction**. The neural
methods instead receive **25 bounded features**: queue/release counts, arm and
finger pose, tool position, the first two targets' presence/position/lateral
position/age, and the five feasibility flags. Neither representation includes
true fruit labels, future arrivals or privileged prototype identities. Tracking
and IK still use simulator geometry. This compares complete representation and
learning pipelines, rather than isolating network architecture alone. The task
learns scheduling and interception, rather than joint torques or visual detection.
These features omit instantaneous velocities and detailed contact state, so the
scheduler has a partial observation of the physical system. Fixed feedback skills
use measured poses; this is not presented as a fully observed physical MDP.

The shared reward is **5 correct − 8 wrong − 6 missed**, minus 0.02 per elapsed
second and 0.25 per failed attempt. Waiting with eligible fruit costs another
0.5 per second. **All RL training and evaluation use the articulated contact
backend**, with no analytic transport or ideal attachment. Training and checkpoint
screening use a class-conditional synthetic prediction-error channel, calibrated
on independent wrist-camera validation crops. Final selection and held-out tests
use actual RGB inference. This remaining perception approximation is explicit;
training is not end-to-end image-based control.

>> Why these learning methods

**Q(λ)** supplies a compact baseline with replacing Watkins eligibility traces
(decay 0.8). **Double DQN** separates online action selection from target-network
valuation, using uniform replay, Huber loss and hard target updates.
[van Hasselt, Guez and Silver (2016)|https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.06461].
**Dueling Double DQN** adds separate value and centered advantage heads, which
can share state-value information across interception choices.
[Wang et al. (2016)|https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.06581].
**PPO** learns a masked categorical actor and value function with a clipped
probability ratio, entropy bonus and generalized advantage estimates.
[Schulman et al. (2017)|https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347].
These methods fit the current discrete option interface. Continuous-control
methods in `examples/nn/6_deep_reinforcement_learning` would require a different
motor-action benchmark.

The neural models share two 32-unit hidden layers: ReLU for Q networks, tanh for
PPO. Native tensors, autograd and Adam implement all learning. DQN uses learning
rate 0.0005, replay capacity 32,768, batch 64, warm-up 512, an update every eight
composite decisions and a target copy every 128 updates. PPO uses learning rate
0.0003, batches of 64, up to 256 rollout decisions, four shuffled epochs,
clipping radius 0.2, GAE decay 0.95, value coefficient 0.5 and entropy coefficient
0.01. PPO also flushes at each window boundary. Q(λ) uses step size 0.08; Q methods
anneal epsilon from 0.35 to 0.01. All use a gradient-norm limit of 10 where applicable.

The discount is **0.995 raised to actual elapsed seconds**. Up to eight trailing
forced-action steps can be composed with exact discounted rewards; every physics
step and arrival still runs. Terminal states stop bootstrapping, while time-limit
boundaries bootstrap from the actual final observation and cut traces across
resets. Saved NPZ files contain complete inference models; optimizer/replay state
is not saved for training resumption.

>> Comparison and selection protocol

Each fresh method trains **256 independent windows of at least 120 simulated
seconds**, with the **same reset-seed schedule** and arrival-rate curriculum
uniform on 0.08–0.30 fruit/s. A final option may overshoot the budget; the history
records actual simulated time and update counts. Initialization is deterministic
and method-specific. This first comparison uses **one training seed per method**
and fixed settings, not a broad hyperparameter search or a statistical claim of
universal algorithm superiority.

Checkpoints at 128 and 256 windows are screened on **12 common
full-physics streams** with synthetic perception. The top two checkpoints per
fresh method are evaluated on **six separate common actual-RGB validation
streams**. The method/checkpoint with the largest **5 correct − 8 wrong − 6 missed**
validation score wins. Checkpoint score ties prefer the earlier checkpoint;
method ties prefer fewer wrong placements, then more correct placements, then
the earlier listed method. Evaluation takes the highest-valued feasible action
(highest actor logit for PPO), with no exploration. All counts are taken at the
exact 120-second boundary.

The old proxy-trained controller is tested using the same current vision, motors,
options, arrivals and score. It previously trained 32,768 windows and selected
window 256; its larger training/search budget and training physics are explicitly different.
Its larger historical budget is shown separately from the matched fresh comparison. The
selection is frozen **before** testing all five candidates on **16 new common
two-minute actual-RGB streams**. Screening seeds start at 63,000,000,000,
validation at 64,000,000,000 and test at 65,000,000,000; these do not overlap with
training, vision datasets or the fixed video seed. Held-out results never change
the selected controller. Wrong placements and pending fruit are retained in the
raw outcomes, alongside correct sorts and misses.

\image(figures/fruit_stream_physics_rl_learning.svg)
\caption(Full-dynamics training returns, averaged in nonoverlapping blocks of 32 windows. Horizontal positions use each method's actual simulated training time, including option overshoot. These returns do not select the deployed model.)
\alttext(Four learning curves show average raw return over the matched training curriculum.)

\image(figures/fruit_stream_physics_rl_selection.svg)
\caption(Actual-RGB validation scores for each fresh pipeline's selected checkpoint and the old proxy-trained reference. Every candidate is eligible for deployment and sees the same six streams; the reference has a different historical training budget.)
\alttext(Five bars compare validation reward based on correct sorts, wrong placements and misses.)

\image(figures/fruit_stream_physics_rl_comparison.svg)
\caption(Held-out full-physics actual-RGB comparison after selection is frozen. All methods see the same sixteen Poisson streams. These results describe this fixed benchmark and training seed.)
\alttext(Two panels compare correct sorts and missed fruit for the four fresh learning pipelines and historical incumbent.)

The fixed validation rule selects **dueling Double DQN, window 256**. On the
sixteen held-out streams it records **111 correct, 8 wrong, 73 missed and 15
pending** from 207 arrivals. The historical controller records 103 correct,
7 wrong, 84 missed and 13 pending on those same streams. These are results for
the new contact task; the earlier attached-payload scores are not directly
comparable. Physical grasp and transfer failures are now represented explicitly.

>> Frozen perception and realistic rendering

The **256×256 rigid wrist camera**, with nominal intrinsics and extrinsics,
supplies rendered RGB. Registered
background subtraction finds visible components, and tracked geometry associates
the intended target. MobileNet receives only the unannotated padded RGB crop;
its prediction alone chooses red or yellow. Empty crops remain unknown, with no
true-class fallback. Video bounding boxes and labels are overlays, never inputs.
The previous classifier initializes a new **12-epoch adaptation** to moving
views from the contact-driven task: 256 training crops from prototypes 0–3,
64 validation crops from prototype 4 and 64 test crops from prototype 5.
These holdouts apply to this adaptation; initial weights retain their previous
simulation-training history.
The frozen adaptation checkpoint was collected during physical-model development
before the arrival-clearance guard; its source fingerprint is retained with the
audit. All reported controller training and evaluation use the final guard.
Validation cross entropy selects the checkpoint. Validation errors, with Laplace
smoothing, set the training prediction channel; test accuracy is reported only
after selection. The resulting classifier is **frozen across all RL pipelines**.

The exterior retains imported Seeed meshes, joint-following cables, procedural
fruit skins, factory scenery and distinct metal, rubber and painted materials.
CUDA can render the overview with 2×2 antialiasing, soft shadows and approximate
studio reflections. The wrist inset and classifier keep the CPU sensor renderer.
Geometry tracking, fixed IK skills, nominal contact parameters and automatic bin
clearing remain benchmark assumptions. This is simulated scheduling and
interception with contact dynamics, not hardware-ready end-to-end control.

\image(figures/fruit_stream_physics_fruit_materials.png)
\caption(Procedural presentation materials. These previews do not enter policy selection.)
\alttext(Apple and banana shape, color and material variants.)

>> Complete executable comparison and demonstration

`just playground` trains the four native pipelines, compares them, freezes the
winner, evaluates and renders it. It adapts the prior wrist classifier to measured pickup views and evaluates
the old controller as a transfer baseline, copying the bundled initial assets on
a clean run. Native asset fetching verifies/downloads the robot CAD as needed.
`DATAMUNGE_STREAM_LEARN_ONLY=1 just playground` stops after comparison.
`just playground-preview` reloads the completed comparison; `just playground-gpu`
uses the CUDA renderer for this frozen replay. Replay validates the protocol,
backend sources, classifier and selected checkpoint hashes and fails on stale
inputs rather than silently using an older run. When changing the comparison
settings in this program, bump its `protocol` string; that string versions the
training/selection settings while backend-source hashes track core changes.

The default video covers world time **40–60 s** of a separately simulated fixed
minute (seed 2,026,113,001), at 15 fps. Counters remain cumulative. This is a
contiguous demonstration excerpt, not additional performance evidence. For quick
render checks use `DATAMUNGE_VIDEO_SECONDS=5 just playground-gpu`; rendering start,
duration, worker count and optional frame dumps retain the existing controls.
The earlier [episodic experiment|file:robot_fruit_episodic.mepml] and
[simple continuing rendering|file:robot_fruit_continuing_simple.mepml] remain
separate reports.

